INSERT INTO articles_table (title, ticker, date, content, summary) VALUES ('The AI Story Just Got Even Bigger', 'NVDA', '2026-06-11 00:00:00', '# NVIDIA: The AI Story Just Got Even Bigger
Jun 11, 2026, 11:30 AM ETNVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) Stock, NVDA:CA Stock, ZNVD:CA Stock
# Summary
Nvidia remains a Strong Buy as AI demand accelerates and the company’s earnings and guidance surpass expectations.
NVDA’s new reporting structure underscores its shift from gaming to AI, highlighting Data Center, Hyperscale, ACIE, and Edge Computing as key growth areas.
Q1 revenues grew 85% year-over-year to $81.6B, with gross margins at 75% and robust momentum in both Hyperscale and ACIE segments.
I see 59% upside to a $318.82 price target, with potential for 130% upside on FY28 earnings, driven by AI infrastructure expansion and enhanced shareholder returns.
# Investment Thesis

NVIDIA (NVDA) remains a Strong Buy despite a recent pullback in semiconductor stocks. AI demand continues to accelerate, and the company’s latest earnings and guidance reinforce my view that Nvidia remains one of the best-positioned beneficiaries of the ongoing AI infrastructure buildout. Since my last report, Nvidia stock has gained 13.3%, outperforming the S&P 500’s 8.5% return, while also exceeding my base-case price target of $235.55, which implied roughly 32% upside at the time.

While some investors have become cautious following the strong rally in AI and semiconductor stocks, I believe the market continues to underestimate both the duration and scale of AI-driven spending. The combination of expanding hyperscaler investments, increasing AI adoption, and Nvidia’s dominant position in accelerated computing provides a strong foundation for continued growth.

As a result, I maintain my Strong Buy rating on Nvidia. In this report, I analyze the company’s latest earnings and guidance, discuss the outlook for AI infrastructure spending, and update my price target.

# Nvidia Aligns Reporting Segment With AI Growth Focus
The importance of artificial intelligence to Nvidia’s business is clear. However, the company has now also embedded that in its reporting structure, which to me highlights that the company expects AI to be a longer-term growth story. The company has shifted from a gaming-heavy business to an AI-heavy business.

The new reporting structure looks as follows:

Data Center
Hyperscale
AI Clouds, Industrial & Enterprise, or ACIE
Edge Computing
It is a break from the company’s prior structure that included Data Center, Gaming, Professional Visualization, Auto, OEM & Other as market segments. The Data Center segment is the AI-heavy segment with Hyperscale, including revenues from public cloud and large internet companies, while ACIE includes purpose-built data centers and AI factors across industries and countries and highlights sovereign AI.

Edge computing includes most of the prior business outside of the Data Center reporting segment and includes the following:

Agentic AI
Physical AI
PCs
Game consoles
Workstations
Base Stations
Robotics
Automotive
# Nvidia AI Growth Continues To Accelerate
Total revenues grew 85% year-over-year and 20% sequentially to $81.6 billion, exceeding estimates by $2.5 billion. Data center sales grew 92% year-on-year and 21% sequentially to $75.2 billion, with 12% sequential growth and 115% year-on-year growth in Hyperscale revenues, and ACIE reported 31% sequential growth and 74% year-on-year growth to $37.4 billion. Edge computing sales grew 10% sequentially and 29% year-on-year to $6.4 billion.

AI demand continues to accelerate with the third consecutive quarter of acceleration in year-on-year growth and the fourteenth quarter of sequential growth. The Blackwell ramp-up is exceeding expectations as Nvidia executes the fastest ramp-up in history to at least somewhat keep up with demand. Previously there still had been the question for some customers whether the company would need the Hopper series for their data centers or the Blackwell series from a cost efficiency and capacity perspective. Naturally, with the Blackwell program ramping up, the Hopper series is gradually phased out, but with demand as robust as we see now, Blackwell is the go-to pick. Furthermore, we note that Hyperscale is not the highest growth area, as we saw ACIE showing nearly triple the sequential growth compared to Hyperscale. It somewhat reduces concerns on Hyperscale growth as we see momentum in AI cloud and sovereign AI as well, and this is a big business for Nvidia, accounting for half of the data center sales, and I believe that AI Clouds could ultimately be the bigger segment within the Data Center group. On Hyperscale, the company is adding Anthropic as a major customer, adding to the growth prospects. Also important to note is that Nvidia is entering the CPU market with their Vera CPU, widening their moat, and networking revenues tripled year-on-year to $15 billion.

In the edge computing business, growth drivers include physical AI with $9 billion in revenues over the past 12 months and robotaxi expansion. Consumer demand was weaker, but that is to be expected with high memory and system prices these days. Consumers are no longer Nvidia’s bread and butter, so it is not a major issue, but I looked for a refresh for my 6-year-old PC, and the price today would be 80%-120% higher than it would be six years ago, and that shows how unaffordable PC refreshes have become for the consumer.
# Nvidia Becomes More Shareholder Return Oriented

For the second quarter, the company expects $91 billion in revenues, indicating 11% sequential growth with gross margins stable at 75% and 9% growth in operating expenses. The company also continues to assume no sales to China.

Key growth drivers are not just the hyperscale growth, but we also note that sovereign AI, agentic AI, and physical AI will drive demand. Nvidia is also attempting to capture a bigger share of the AI infrastructure pie with its Vera CPU and networking solutions. Nvidia is increasingly transforming from a GPU to a broader AI infrastructure solutions provider.

That AI infrastructure pie is growing, as became clear during the earnings call:

With analysts now forecasting hyperscale CapEx to exceed $1 trillion in 2027 and agentic AI beginning to proliferate all industries, AI infrastructure spending is on track to reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by the end of this decade.

On the topic of shareholder returns, Nvidia has increased its buyback authorization by $80 billion, bringing the total available authorization to $119 billion. Furthermore, the company increased its dividend from $0.01 to $0.25 and targets to return 50% of free cash flow to shareholders. It is a major shift from a pure growth company toward a growth-plus-shareholder returns company.

# Nvidia Upside Remains Strong as Earnings Expectations Rise Even Further
To determine multi-year price targets, The Aerospace Forum has developed a stock screener that uses a combination of analyst consensus on EBITDA, cash flows, and the most recent balance sheet data. Each quarter, we revisit those assumptions and update the stock price targets accordingly. In a separate blog, I have detailed our analysis methodology.

For Nvidia, I believe that there is a 59% upside to the stock price with a $318.82 price target, and that is arguably a conservative target driven by current year earnings against the peer group valuation, which is how Nvidia stock is also currently valued. If we were to pull in the FY28 earnings as I typically look at earnings one year ahead, we would get a $467.96 price target, indicating more than 130% upside.

Incorporated in my new price targets are:

The increased dividend as part of the 50% return of free cash flow to shareholders.
An 11% increase in EBITDA estimates for 2027-2029 with growth expectations increased by 7% this year, 18% next year, and 11% for 2029.
A 20% increase in free cash flow estimates for 2027-2029 with growth expectations increased by 19% this year, 21% next year, and 27% for 2029.

My EBITDA margin projections are currently expecting growth to be around 65%-68%, which is actually a slight reduction from the 72% I modeled earlier, driven by continued and accelerated investments in new product development. Cash conversion ratios are remaining strong as capital expenditures remain modest and the business continues to grow. Shareholder returns show that yield on cost is not going to look great for the years to come, but this is a stock that has substantial growth ahead, so it is not surprising to see yield on cost being poor. While substantially increasing shareholder purchases in the years ahead, we also see that the company will grow its net cash position. So, over time, I actually also hope to see Nvidia strategically deploying more than the 50% free cash flow target for shareholder returns.

# Conclusion: Nvidia Growth Keeps Impressing
Nvidia is becoming more and more of an AI growth story with upward pressure, and what is particularly notable is that the company has altered its reporting structure to reflect the business’ growth areas, and we are also seeing strong sovereign growth. Whereas this was mostly a hyperscale growth story and sales to China were happening or not happening, we are now seeing hyperscale growth, sovereign growth, and the third pillar of growth is likely going to be in the edge computing part of the business with agentic and physical AI growth. With Nvidia’s stock having recently sold off just like that of peers while expectations have actually improved, I maintain my strong buy rating.

', '
Nvidia remains a Strong Buy as AI demand accelerates and the company’s earnings and guidance surpass expectations.
NVDA’s new reporting structure underscores its shift from gaming to AI, highlighting Data Center, Hyperscale, ACIE, and Edge Computing as key growth areas.
Q1 revenues grew 85% year-over-year to $81.6B, with gross margins at 75% and robust momentum in both Hyperscale and ACIE segments.
I see 59% upside to a $318.82 price target, with potential for 130% upside on FY28 earnings, driven by AI infrastructure expansion and enhanced shareholder returns.
');
INSERT INTO articles_table (title, ticker, date, content, summary) VALUES ('The Pullback Is A Gift As AI Demand Goes Parabolic (Rating Upgrade)', 'NVDA', '2026-06-11 00:00:00', '# Nvidia: The Pullback Is A Gift As AI Demand Goes Parabolic (Rating Upgrade)
Jun 11, 2026, 10:39 AM ETNVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) Stock, NVDA:CA Stock, ZNVD:CA StockOPENAI, META, META
# Summary
Nvidia Corporation demonstrates accelerating commercial momentum, posting $81.6 billion in Q1 FY27 revenue, up 85% YoY, and guiding Q2 revenue to $91 billion.
NVDA''s AI infrastructure opportunity is repeatedly underestimated, with consensus FY27 revenue expectations doubling over two years to ~$393–395 billion.
Multi-year demand visibility is reinforced by hyperscaler capex plans, supply-chain pre-commitments, and strategic partnerships like OpenAI''s 10-gigawatt buildout.
Despite recent share weakness, NVDA enjoys strong forward visibility through 2028, with risks centered on potential future industry overbuild rather than current demand.
I value NVDA stock using the residual earnings model and calculate an implied share price target of ~$265.

# Investment Thesis

The AI-driven bull run in semiconductor stocks has hit pause over the past few weeks, with NVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) shares dropping >10% from the ATH made in mid-April. The current weakness is, in my view, a potentially attractive accumulation opportunity, as Nvidia''s commercial momentum continues to accelerate.

# NVIDIA''s Fundamentals Show No Sign Of Slowing
To bring Nvidia''s momentum into perspective, in Q1 FY 2027, the company generated $81.6 billion of revenue, up 85% YoY and 20% sequentially. Within that, Data Center revenue reached $75.2 billion, up 92% YoY and 21% sequentially. This matters because Nvidia is no longer compounding off a small base. It is adding more than $10 billion of sequential revenue in a single quarter while sustaining gross margins around 75%.

Even more importantly, management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, implying another double-digit sequential step-up. Notably, the guide also excludes China Data Center compute revenue, which means the underlying demand signal is strong enough to absorb a major export-restricted geography and still push the company toward a roughly $360 billion annualized revenue run-rate.

# with markets continuously discovering that Nvidia''s AI infrastructure opportunity is larger than previously assumed
What I find more impressive is that Nvidia''s commercial momentum is underwritten by a still-ongoing upward revision cycle. For context, over the past two years, FY 2027 revenue expectations have effectively doubled, with consensus revenue now around $393–395 billion. The consensus expectation has moved higher in repeated step-changes, especially around earnings events, suggesting that each reporting cycle is forcing the market to rebase Nvidia''s future revenue power upward.

The key implication is that the market is continuously discovering that Nvidia''s AI infrastructure opportunity is larger, more visible, and more concentrated in high-demand data center workloads than previously assumed. In my view, as long as revenue estimates keep moving higher, the stock can continue to find support even after a strong run.

Management commentary supports the same conclusion. Jensen Huang described the buildout of AI factories as “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history” and said it is accelerating at extraordinary speed. On the Q1 call, he went further, saying demand had “gone parabolic” because agentic AI is now doing productive work, tokens are becoming profitable, and model developers are racing to produce more compute. Furthermore, Nvidia''s demand is no longer only about training larger models. The revenue pool is broadening toward inference, reasoning, agents, synthetic data, and robotics. That changes the buyer psychology: customers are not simply optimizing chip budgets but securing scarce productive capacity.

# Demand becomes not only stronger but also more “visible”
There is strong evidence of improving demand visibility, as the CAPEX cycle is extending beyond Blackwell into the next architecture generation. Management has said Vera Rubin should begin ramping in the second half of 2026, and CEO Jensen Huang added that he expects Nvidia to remain supply-constrained throughout Vera Rubin''s entire life.

The OpenAI (OPENAI) partnership announced back in September 2025 reinforces this point. Nvidia and OpenAI announced a strategic plan to deploy at least 10 gigawatts of Nvidia systems, representing millions of GPUs, with the first gigawatt expected to come online in the second half of 2026 on Vera Rubin. Nvidia also intends to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI progressively as each gigawatt is deployed. The scale is important: one gigawatt is already a massive AI infrastructure commitment; 10 gigawatts is a multi-year buildout program. It also directly links Nvidia''s roadmap to one of the most aggressive frontier-model customers in the market.

Hyperscaler commentary points in the same direction. Microsoft Corporation (MSFT) said it added another gigawatt of capacity in the quarter and remains on track to double its data center footprint in two years, while continuing to modernize its fleet with the latest Nvidia and Advanced Micro Devices, Inc. (AMD) systems. Alphabet Inc. (GOOG, GOOGL) reported Q1 CapEx of $35.7 billion, largely directed toward AI technical infrastructure, while Google Cloud revenue grew 63% YoY to $20 billion, and backlog nearly doubled sequentially to $462 billion. More importantly, management raised 2026 CapEx guidance to $180–190 billion and said 2027 CapEx should increase significantly again.

Amazon.com, Inc. (AMZN) and Meta Platforms, Inc. (META) tell the same story. AWS revenue grew 28% YoY to $37.6 billion, its fastest growth in 15 quarters, while Amazon said higher property and equipment purchases were primarily driven by AI investments. The company also disclosed plans to deploy more than one million Nvidia GPUs starting in 2026, while Bedrock processed more tokens in Q1 than in all prior years combined, and customer spend rose 170% sequentially. Meta, meanwhile, raised its 2026 CapEx outlook to $125–145 billion, citing higher component pricing and additional data center costs to support future-year capacity.

The supply chain data supports the same conclusion. Nvidia disclosed $119 billion of manufacturing, supply, and capacity commitments as of April 2026, with $95 billion payable through the remainder of FY2027 and the balance extending into FY2028–FY2031. This is a hard financial signal that Nvidia is securing supply across both current and future architectures. TSMC has also continued to describe AI demand as difficult to satisfy.

Lastly, memory commentary further extends the visibility window. SK Hynix has said its 2026 DRAM, high-bandwidth memory (“HBM”), and NAND output is sold out, while Micron Technology, Inc. (MU) has indicated that HBM demand is already committed under multi-year agreements. Micron also sees the HBM market expanding from roughly $35 billion in 2025 to around $100 billion by 2028. This matters because HBM is one of the core constraints in AI accelerator systems. If memory supply is sold out, customers are signing multi-year agreements, and hyperscalers are still raising capex plans into 2027, then AI infrastructure buyers are not behaving as if this is a short-cycle inventory build. They are behaving as if compute will remain strategically scarce for several more years.

# Valuation still has upside
I value Nvidia using the residual earnings model: For Nvidia, I use EPS estimates aligned with analyst consensus through FY 2031, and for the terminal value, I apply a 9% cost of equity and 3% terminal growth. This gives me an implied target price of ~$265 per share.

# A note on risks
In my view, the main risk for Nvidia is not demand size or visibility today but the potential for this demand to be excessive vs. economics: if AI infrastructure capacity grows faster than monetization, hyperscaler CapEx could slow, and Nvidia''s visibility would compress. At the same time, competition is another risk. Custom silicon, AMD, and other accelerators may not displace Nvidia, but they could pressure pricing and margins over time. Finally, expectations are already high. Any slowdown in estimate revisions, export tightening, or weaker AI ROI could pressure the multiple, even if Nvidia remains the clear market leader.

# Investor takeaway
My conclusion is that Nvidia''s momentum has become more visible, not less visible, despite the enormous absolute scale of the business. The company has current-period acceleration, next-quarter guidance acceleration, customer capex acceleration, supply-chain pre-commitments, architecture-level visibility into Vera Rubin, and multi-year demand signals from frontier AI customers. The risk is not that demand is weak today.

The real debate is whether the industry overbuilds later. But based on the evidence available now, the market is still dealing with a system where demand is being pulled forward by profitable inference, agentic workloads, and enterprise adoption, while supply remains constrained across chips, memory, packaging, power, and data centers. That gives Nvidia unusually strong visibility through 2028. I rate Nvidia Buy with a ~$265 per share target price.

', '
Nvidia Corporation demonstrates accelerating commercial momentum, posting $81.6 billion in Q1 FY27 revenue, up 85% YoY, and guiding Q2 revenue to $91 billion.
NVDA''s AI infrastructure opportunity is repeatedly underestimated, with consensus FY27 revenue expectations doubling over two years to ~$393–395 billion.
Multi-year demand visibility is reinforced by hyperscaler capex plans, supply-chain pre-commitments, and strategic partnerships like OpenAI''s 10-gigawatt buildout.
Despite recent share weakness, NVDA enjoys strong forward visibility through 2028, with risks centered on potential future industry overbuild rather than current demand.
I value NVDA stock using the residual earnings model and calculate an implied share price target of ~$265.
