INSERT INTO articles_table (title, ticker, date, content, summary) VALUES ('Nvidia Looks Undervalued, But That Doesn''t Make Me More Bullish', 'NVDA', '2026-06-10 00:00:00', '# Nvidia Looks Undervalued, But That Doesn''t Make Me More Bullish
Jun 10, 2026, 2:07 PM ETNVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) Stock, ZNVD:CA Stock, NVDA:CA StockFXCOF
# Summary
Nvidia Corporation trades at a 1-yr fwd P/E below its history and peers. Its implied growth ask rate is also undemanding.
Revenue growth near 85% could persist as Nvidia expands into CPUs. And Foxconn supplier data indicates strong AI demand ahead.
Falling GPU rental prices and entry into low-margin consumer PCs could pressure Nvidia’s currently high gross margins.
Growing adoption of custom AI chips by hyperscalers like Amazon and Google can threaten Nvidia’s long-term pricing power and data center share.
NVDA''s chart shows a failed breakout and a return to a wide trading range, suggesting choppy, sideways price action despite strong fundamentals.
# Performance assessment
Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) has slightly lagged the broader market index since my last update on the stock due to a correction kicking in recently:
# Elevator pitch
NVDA stock''s valuations look attractive, and its revenue growth prospects still look very healthy. But there are also some risks that are germinating quietly:

Very high revenue growth can sustain for longer.
Falls in GPU rental prices and a foray into the PC market can weigh down on gross margins.
A shift toward custom chips by hyperscalers is a risk to watch.
NVDA stock looks undervalued on multiple measures.
A failed break above resistance pops prices back into a big weekly range.
# Very high revenue growth can sustain for longer
Nvidia is targeting the PC market more aggressively with its Vera CPUs, which are ramping up more from cloud partners in H2 CY26:


Building on the success of our Grace CPU, Vera is arriving just in time to meet this inflection... Vera will deliver up to 1.5x faster performance per core, 2x performance per watt and 4x density per rack compared to x86-based alternatives. Vera CPU opens a brand-new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before, and every major hyperscale and system maker is partnering with us to get it deployed. We have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world''s leading CPU supplier.

- CFO Colette Kress in the Q1 FY27 earnings call.

To give you a sense of scale, the $20B in incremental CPU revenue this year amounts to an ~8% uplift to TTM revenues of $253B.

Another good sign is the fact that Foxconn''s (FXCOF) monthly revenue trend is accelerating nicely to such a level that the May month this year has already reached the previous years'' Sep-Nov''s seasonal peak levels

The key driver of Foxconn''s revenue acceleration here is strong momentum for its cloud and networking AI products. Overall, this is a positive leading indicator for Nvidia, as Foxconn (also called Hon Hai Precision Industry) sells more than 50% of the latter''s AI input hardware components.

Due to these revenue tailwinds, I anticipate that Nvidia will continue growing its revenues at very high 85%+ rates for the next couple of quarters:

# Falls in GPU rental prices and a foray into the PC market can weigh down on gross margins
The GPU compute rental prices for H100 (the most mature GPU deployed widely across hyperscalers) and B200 (the newer, high-performance GPU) have been stagnant to falling:
This puts pressure on the companies leasing out GPUs, such as Oracle (ORCL), which some reports say is earning a fairly thin 14% margin in this business. I think falling GPU rental yields can eventually pressure Nvidia to make some concessions on the premium pricing for its AI chips. This can be a headwind to its high 75% gross margins.

Also, I note that Nvidia is foraying into the consumer PC market with the RTX Spark superchip. Now, the consumer PC business has high competition, is commoditized, and as a result has lower single-digit type margins. So revenue toward consumer hardware can dilute the company''s overall gross margins profile.

For these reasons, I believe we may see some gross margin erosion ahead:

# A shift toward custom chips by hyperscalers is a risk to watch
Among the hyperscalers, Amazon (AMZN) and Alphabet/Google (GOOGL) have the highest proportion of their own, custom AI chips:
And interestingly, these 2 hyperscalers also have the highest AI capex ROIs (before costs), according to my estimates:

In fact, I believe Google''s ROIs are understated here. Read this article for more details.

Unsurprisingly, a company''s own internal chips lead to better economics. And in a recent piece on Broadcom (AVGO), I discussed in more detail why I believe custom AI chips may become more of a trend among hyperscalers. You can read that piece here.

Ultimately, I view this as another medium- to longer-term risk for Nvidia''s pricing power and future market share.

# NVDA stock looks undervalued on multiple measures
NVDA is at a 1-yr fwd P/E of 20.9x, which is 34% below the longer-term average of 31.8x:

Dissecting the drivers of the stock, NVDA''s earnings multiple has been rising rapidly, whilst its 1-yr fwd P/E valuation multiples have been contracting:
This means the stock''s near-term fundamental outlook is still very robust. It is only being held back by the more sentiment-driven factor of the valuation multiple''s ebbs and flows.

Relative to some semiconductor comps, NVDA stock is trading at a 28% discount to the 29.2x 1-yr fwd P/E median of its comps:

This is not normal for NVDA, as it typically has traded at a 3% premium to its peers:

If we look at the implied earnings growth ask rate for NVDA stock, we get a 16.75% 7-yr CAGR, assuming no change in the exit multiple from the current levels of 21x:

Now, the current normalized earnings growth track is much higher at close to 80% YoY:

And consensus forward earnings estimates imply an overall 7-yr CAGR of 27%:

This is much higher than the required ~17% 7-yr earnings CAGR.

So the bottom line is that NVDA stock looks undervalued from multiple analysis perspectives

# A failed break above resistance pops prices back into a big weekly range

If this is your first time reading a Hunting Alpha article using Technical Analysis, you may want to read this post, which explains how and why I read the charts the way I do. All my charts reflect total shareholder return as they are adjusted for dividends/distributions.
## NVDA Technical Analysis
NVDA stock attempted a breakout above a broad weekly range''s resistance level. But this failed as the prices have printed a rejection at range resistance. Thus, I view the market state to be rangebound. Hence, I think we will see more ebbs and flows but overall sideways action ahead.

# Takeaway & Positioning
Fundamental Thesis: Neutral since, despite strong revenue growth, there are margin deterioration and competition risks to watch.
Valuation: Bullish as NVDA looks undervalued from multiple perspectives.
Technical Analysis: Neutral as a failed breakout above range resistance makes the market state range-bound.
Final Assessment and Rating: Neutral, Hold.
